"CONSUMER CONTRACTS\ninterest of the party that drafted it\"); Gray v. Am. Exp. Co., 743 F.2d 10, 18 (D.C. Cir. 1984)\n(noting that \"in choosing among interpretations, meaning which operates against drafters is\npreferred\"); Lechmere Tire & Sales Co. v. Burwick, 277 N.E.2d 503, 506 (Mass. 1972) (holding\nthat the credit-card agreement at issue should be construed against the drafter and \"that all doubts\nas to its interpretation and as to the meaning of the card itself are to be resolved in favor of the\napplicant for the card\"). Illustration 1 is based on Masonic Acc. Ins. Co. v. Jackson, 164 N.E. 628\n(Ind. 1929).\nThe same logic applies to the effect given to language used by the business in structuring\nthe process of contract formation and adoption of terms. Through this language, the business\ninstructs consumers how to form the contract and adopt the terms, and provides notices regarding\nthe legal consequences of specific acts. When this guidance is susceptible to more than one\nreasonable understanding, that which operates against the business-drafter is preferred. For\nexample, a business may instruct the consumer that clicking on an \"I Agree\" button would result\nin the formation of a contract and adoption of some standard contract terms, but the language and\ndesign of this process may result in uncertainty about which sets of standard contract terms are\nadopted. Accordingly, when evaluating whether adoption processes, notices, disclosures, and\nproposed modifications are reasonably communicated to consumers, courts require that the\nlanguage alerting consumers to the specific steps that will result in the adoption or modification of\nstandard contract terms be stated in a clear manner and that ambiguous language be interpreted\nagainst the drafter. See § 2, Comment 8; § 3, Comment 3; Sgouros v. Transunion Corp., 817 F.3d\n1029 (7th Cir. 2016) (courts \"need ... to look more closely at both the law and the facts to see if\na reasonable person in Sgouros's shoes would have realized that he was assenting to the Service\nAgreement\"); Lee v. Intelius Inc. 737 F.3d 1254 (9th Cir. 2013) (when the statement by the\nbusiness about the consequences of the consumer clicking a particular button does not clearly state\nthat additional terms are adopted, the court constructed it as insufficient to result in the adoption\nof the terms). And, when the meaning of the notice stating which packets of standard contract\nterms are adopted by a particular act is not clear, a meaning that operates against the business is\npreferred. Illustration 2 is based on Sgouros v. Transunion Corp., 817 F.3d 1029 (7th Cir. 2016).\nd. Reasonable expectations. When a standard contract term is susceptible to more than one\nreasonable meaning, courts have adopted the meaning that is consistent with the reasonable\nexpectations of consumers, and have declined to bind consumers to any meaning that conflicts\nwith such expectations. See Dorchester Mut. Ins. Co. v. Krusell, 150 N.E.3d 731, 738 (Mass. 2020)\n(after concluding that the relevant term in the insurance policy is ambiguous, the court proceeds\nto consider \"what an objectively reasonable insured, reading the relevant policy language, would\nexpect to be covered\"); James B. Nutter & Co. v. Est. of Murphy, 88 N.E.3d 1133, 1139 (Mass.\n2018) (stating that when interpreting ambiguous language in adhesion contracts, the court seeks to\neffectuate \"the meaning an objectively reasonable person in the nondrafting party's position would\ngive to the language in the contract\"; and quoting Restatement of the Law Second, Contracts § 211,\nComment e: \"courts in construing and applying a standardized contract seek to effectuate the\nreasonable expectations of the average member of the public who accepts it.\"); Golchin v. Liberty\n93\n@§4\n"